            <ArticleTitle>Surgical Management of Metabolic Syndrome Related to Morbid Obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>145-58, x</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pop.2015.10.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0095-4543(15)00100-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Current treatment approaches in morbid obesity are multimodal in nature. Combination therapies include increases in moderate-intensity aerobic and resistance exercise; behavioral lifestyle changes to increase compliance with diet and activity recommendations; medical nutrition therapy; intensive medical therapy; and metabolic surgical procedures, such as gastric bypass and vertical sleeve gastrectomy. This article focuses on the preoperative evaluation and proper patient selection for metabolic surgery. The procedures are discussed relative to their anatomy, metabolic mechanism of action, and common adverse effects.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
